  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{\u2218})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx={720}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,\\,x=a+{720}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{\u2218},{180}^{\u2218}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{\u2218}$",
  "worked_solution": "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{\u2218} & ={150}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}+{150}^{\u2218} & ={210}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={150}^{\u2218}-{150}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={210}^{\u2218}-{150}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & ={360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={60}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={720}^{\u2218}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={120}^{\u2218}+{720}^{\u2218}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={120}^{\u2218}.$",
